       <h3>Backtracking</h3>
-      <p><strong>backtracking</strong> is an advanced solving technique — and the method computer solvers use when simple deduction is not enough. You pick an undecided cell, assume it holds a bulb, and propagate all consequences. If you reach a contradiction, your assumption was wrong and that cell cannot hold a bulb. If no contradiction appears, you continue until the puzzle is solved. Human solvers rarely need formal backtracking on Easy or Medium puzzles, but Hard puzzles on larger grids may require it. The GridPaw <a href="/akari/solver/">Akari solver</a> uses backtracking as its final resolution step.</p>
