       <h2>Understand Line of Sight</h2>
       <p>Every bulb illuminates its own cell and every cell along its row and column in both directions, stopping at the first black wall. Two consequences matter constantly:</p>
       <ol>
         <li><strong>A bulb can never see another bulb.</strong> If placing one would light a cell that already contains a bulb, that placement is illegal. This is the rule that makes elimination work.</li>
-        <li><strong>Walls cast shadows.</strong> A cell in the shadow of a wall on both axes must be lit by something local — usually a forced placement nearby.</li>
+        <li><strong>Walls cast shadows.</strong> A cell in the shadow of a wall on both axes must be lit by something local, usually a forced placement nearby.</li>
       </ol>
       <p>Once line of sight is automatic, the <a href="/akari/tips/elimination/">elimination technique</a> becomes natural: instead of asking "where can a bulb go?", ask "what breaks if I put one here?"</p>
 
       <h2>Work From Constraints, Not From Empty Space</h2>
